# bor-sec-32 — Yakobi, Ofir, James Boylan, and James Danckert, "Behavioral and Electroencephalographic Evidence for Reduced Attentional Control and Performance Monitoring in Boredom" (2021)

**Type:** empirical EEG + behavioral study (resting-state recording + go/no-go sustained-attention task) · **Discipline:** cognitive/affective neuroscience and psychophysiology · **Depth:** deep · **Corpus role:** Strand D — EEG/neurophysiology of boredom; sibling to bor-sec-27 (Barry et al., eyes-open/closed baseline methodology), bor-sec-29 (Miyauchi & Kawasaki, task-unrelated-thought EEG), bor-sec-30 (Perone et al., frontal alpha asymmetry), bor-sec-31 (Seo et al., ML classification), and bor-sec-33 (Yuvaraj et al., EEG functional connectivity); `grounds-measure`-bridges to the not-yet-built *Boredom Experiment (VR Attention Study)* dataset's behavioral-performance-monitoring and EEG channels.

## 1. Thesis/Aim

Yakobi, Boylan, and Danckert (*Psychophysiology* 58.6, 2021, e13816) record EEG from 83–86 University of Waterloo undergraduates during a resting-state protocol and a go/no-go sustained-attention task to test whether state (task-induced) and trait (boredom-proneness) boredom are associated with reduced attentional control and performance monitoring, at both behavioral and electrophysiological levels (FE, ~p.1). Building on prior findings that boredom proneness (BP) predicts attentional lapses and diminished performance monitoring (Malkovsky et al., 2012; Hunter & Eastwood, 2018), the authors predicted that both trait and state boredom would correlate negatively with (a) task performance, (b) the amplitude of the stimulus-locked P3 event-related potential (attentional-resource allocation), and (c) the amplitude of the response-locked error-related negativity (ERN; performance monitoring) (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.2). A second, exploratory aim examined whether trait BP related to resting-state EEG power in the theta, alpha, and beta bands (FE, ~p.2). The authors conclude that "state and trait boredom are associated with inadequate engagement of attentional resources" (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.1) — but via dissociable routes: state boredom predicts task-evoked performance and brain activity, while trait BP predicts only spontaneous resting-state oscillatory power, with no measurable effect on task performance, the ERPs, or task-induced boredom itself (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.8).

## 2. Structure

Twelve published pages across four numbered sections plus author contributions, references, and a methodological appendix; anchors follow the article's own page-footers ("1 of 12"–"12 of 12"), given as approximate since the extracted column order is occasionally interleaved.

1. **Abstract & keywords** (~p.1).
2. **Introduction** (~pp.1–3) — boredom's definition (Eastwood et al., 2012) and functional/regulatory framing (Danckert, 2019; Kurzban et al., 2013), footnoting the multidimensionality debate and aligning with Elpidorou (2020) on unidimensionality; trait-BP correlates (ADHD symptomatology, SART lapses, Golubchik et al.'s 2020 methylphenidate study); the sparse EEG-boredom literature (Katahira et al., 2018; Tabatabaie et al., 2014); P3/ERN as candidate indices, situated against Núñez Castellar et al.'s (2019) task-difficulty-confounded oddball study; hypotheses.
3. **Method** (~pp.3–4) — Participants (86, one excluded); Materials (go/no-go task with adaptive SOA; Short BP Scale; pre/post state-boredom probes; task-demand rating; 32-site Biosemi EEG; resting-state band extraction via individual alpha frequency; P3/ERN extraction windows); Procedure (~80-min fixed-morning session).
4. **Results** (~pp.4–8) — descriptives (Table 1); behavioral regressions (commission errors and task demand predict task-induced boredom, BP does not; Figure 2 SOA asymptote; Figure 3 posttask-only block correlations); ERP regressions (Table 2; task-induced boredom predicts reduced P3/ERN beyond demand/BP/errors; Figures 4–5); resting-state LMM (Table 3; BP × condition × band interaction, effect confined to eyes-open; Figure 6); task-rest contextualization (resting alpha vs. ERN magnitude; P3 vs. occipital theta/alpha).
5. **Discussion** (~pp.8–10) — replication/extension of prior behavioral work; the null BP–performance/ERP link and three candidate explanations; P3 via context-updating theory (Donchin & Coles, 1988) and task-difficulty/resource-allocation modulators (Polich, 2003, 2007); ERN via ACC cost-benefit-control theory (Shenhav et al., 2013); P3/ERN mutual non-correlation implying distinct executive mechanisms; the posttask-only pattern as cautious evidence disengagement precedes state boredom; resting alpha/theta via the inhibition-timing hypothesis (Klimesch et al., 2007) and eyes-open-specific DMN/alpha coupling (Mo et al., 2013); explicit rejection of "a simple mechanism of reduced attention."
6. **Author contributions, ORCID, references** (~pp.10–12).
7. **Appendix A: EEG processing pipeline** (~p.12) — downsampling, filtering, ICA/ICLabel artifact removal, epoching, and band/ERP extraction parameters.

## 3. Constructs

- **profound/deep boredom (FCM 3rd form) / being-bored-by (1st form) / being-bored-with (2nd form)** — not treated; no Heideggerian vocabulary appears anywhere in this psychophysiology article.
- **situational vs. trait/state boredom** — the paper's central dissociation and main finding: state ("task-induced") boredom, operationalized as the posttask-minus-pretask self-report delta, predicts task performance, P3, and ERN, while trait BP predicts none of these — it "had no influence on either behavior or brain activity during a sustained attention task nor did trait BP influence subjective reports of task-induced boredom and task demands" (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.8). BP's only significant correlate is resting-state EEG power.
- **boredom proneness** — Short BP Scale (Struk et al., 2017; 8-item; N=86); negatively predicts resting alpha (B=−0.13, p=.015) and theta (B=−0.11, p=.035) power, eyes-open only (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.7); its null relation to performance/state boredom is attributed to task self-adjustment, EEG-lab novelty, and restricted BP range in the sample (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.8).
- **mind-wandering / task-unrelated thought** — not measured; invoked only as an interpretive DMN/alpha citation chain in the Discussion (FE, ~p.9–10). Directly operationalized instead in sibling unit bor-sec-29 (Miyauchi & Kawasaki).
- **attentional disengagement / meta-awareness** — the foundational construct, adopted from Eastwood et al. (2012); operationalized via commission/omission errors and P3/ERN amplitude.
- **boredom-as-regulatory-signal (functional account)** — invoked via Danckert (2019, exploration/exploitation balance) and Kurzban et al. (2013, opportunity-cost signaling) (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.1); see §7 for the honest cross-check against this cluster's Elpidorou units.
- **flow / engagement (contrast pole)** — discussed only via citation to Katahira et al.'s (2018) three-condition EEG study (boredom < flow/overload in alpha, theta); not part of this study's own design.
- **monotony / meaninglessness / temporality of boredom (time-drag)** — not thematized as explicit constructs; the go/no-go paradigm's repetitive structure is the implicit induction method, and "task-induced boredom" is itself a temporal delta score, but neither vocabulary appears in the text.

## 4. Measures & methods

**Participants.** 86 undergraduates (64 female; M age=19.84, SD=1.44); one excluded for using the wrong response key, leaving N=85 for behavioral analyses. Of the 85, 3 lost EEG data (technical error, N=82 valid EEG); 4 more were excluded from the ERN analysis for artifacts (N=78 ERN, N=82 P3); separately, 3 were excluded from resting-state analysis for >30% artifact rate (N=79) (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.3, Figure 1).

**Go/no-go task** (Garavan et al., 1999, 2002): 600 trials/10 blocks, rapid (200 ms) letters/digits, respond to all but immediate repeats ("no-go," ~30% of trials); SOA adaptively adjusted (800–1,100 ms, ±50 ms) to hold commission-error rate near 30% for reliable error-ERP elicitation (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.3).

**Short BP Scale** (Struk et al., 2017, 8-item, 1–7 Likert); **state-boredom probes** (1–9 slider, pre/post task; task-induced = post−pre); **task demand** (1–100 slider, post-task only) (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.3–4).

**EEG apparatus:** Biosemi Active-Two, active Ag/AgCl electrodes, 32 sites (10–20 system), linked-mastoid reference, horizontal/vertical EOG; 2,048 Hz sampling, downsampled to 256 Hz. **Resting-state:** 2 min eyes-closed + 2 min eyes-open at session start (only the first recording analyzed); individual alpha frequency (IAF) via Corcoran et al.'s (2018) method; power on 1-s epochs (Welch/FFT, 0.25 Hz resolution) in theta/alpha/beta, defined relative to IAF per Appendix A (theta=IAF−5, alpha=IAF, beta=IAF+6) (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.4, 12). **ERPs:** ERN = response-locked, 0–100 ms (error−correct), midfrontal electrode per the Method text (Fz) — though Table 2's own column header reads "ERN (Cz)," a minor source-internal inconsistency reported here rather than silently resolved. P3 = stimulus-locked, 400–600 ms post-no-go-stimulus, midparietal (Pz) (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.4).

**Procedure:** fixed 11:00 a.m. start, ~80 min; resting-state EEG, then the Balloon Analogue Risk Task (not reported here) and go/no-go in randomized order; go/no-go framed by the two boredom probes and the task-demand item (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.4).

**Against the controlled MEASURE axis: behavioral (RT/errors/performance-monitoring)** — go/no-go task above (N=85); commission errors positively predicted task-induced boredom (B=6.11, p=.006; omission errors and BP n.s.; task demand negative, B=−0.045, p<.001; model F(4,80)=6.58, p<.001); longer mean SOA (poorer performance) also predicted higher task-induced boredom (B=0.014, p=.011) (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.5). **Self-report/survey scale** — BP scale, probes, and demand item above. **EEG alpha/theta power** — resting-state spectral power (N=79); BP negatively predicts alpha/theta in eyes-open rest only; alpha negatively relates to ERN magnitude (B=−0.047, p=.008) and P3 relates positively to occipital (Oz) theta/alpha (B=0.038, p=.036) (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.7–8). **EEG eyes-open vs. eyes-closed** — fixed factor in the resting-state LMM; BP-power effects confined to eyes-open (Table 3). **EEG event-related potentials (P3, ERN)** — not itself on the controlled axis; nearest neighbors are "EEG alpha/theta power" (oscillatory) and "behavioral...performance-monitoring" (functional, esp. ERN): task-induced boredom significantly predicted reduced P3 (B=−0.394, p=.047, N=82) and reduced ERN magnitude (B=0.286, p=.025, N=78), each beyond demand/errors/BP (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.6). **Blink/eye-closure (EOG)** — recorded solely for ICA-based ocular-artifact correction, never analyzed as a substantive boredom DV.

Not treated: **EEG frontal alpha asymmetry** (power-based, not laterality-based, alpha measure — see bor-sec-30); **EEG functional connectivity** (no coherence analysis — see bor-sec-33); **pupil dilation/pupillometry** and **gaze variance/fixation dispersion** (no eye-tracking — see bor-sec-28); **heart rate/HRV**; **predictive-processing model**; **ML classification** (regression/LMM only — see bor-sec-31).

## 5. Findings/Claims

- Boredom is defined, following Eastwood et al. (2012), as "the unfulfilled desire to be engaged in a satisfying mental activity," an aversive state "characterized by poor self-regulation" (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.1).
- State (task-induced) boredom is predicted by commission errors (positive) and perceived task demand (negative), independent of BP; omission errors show no relationship (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.5).
- Longer mean SOA (a proxy for poorer performance) also predicts higher task-induced boredom (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.5).
- Only posttask, not pretask, boredom correlates with block-level performance, replicating Hunter and Eastwood's (2018) method — "failures of attention precede state boredom" within a session (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.5).
- Task-induced boredom predicts reduced P3 and ERN amplitude beyond task demands, errors, and BP; BP has no significant effect on either ERP (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.6).
- The posttask-only correlation pattern recurs for both ERPs across task halves, paralleling the behavioral finding (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.6–7).
- BP negatively predicts resting alpha/theta power in eyes-open, not eyes-closed, rest; error rate does not improve the resting-state model, and task-induced boredom shows no relationship to resting EEG (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.7).
- P3 and ERN were mutually uncorrelated, read as evidence of "distinct processes related to executive functioning: disruption in working-memory updating and reduced performance monitoring," not one unitary mechanism (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.9).
- The authors explicitly reject "a simple mechanism of reduced attention," since that "would predict higher levels of misses, rather than false-alarms — as opposed to the pattern found here" (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.9–10); the effect is specific to commission errors/performance monitoring, not generic attentional shutdown.
- Conclusion: both trait and state boredom "may reflect maladaptive or suboptimal engagement of attentional resources," via dissociable pathways — trait tied to spontaneous brain activity, state tied to task-evoked activity (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.10).

