## 7. Analytical role

*(anticipatory-application)* On the specific check this entry was asked to make honestly: this source does **not** cite either of this cluster's Elpidorou regulatory-theory units. Footnote 1 (~p.1) cites Elpidorou (2020), "Is Boredom One or Many?", for the unidimensionality debate only — a different paper, on a different question, published after bor-sec-25 (2018a, "The Bored Mind Is a Guiding Mind") and bor-sec-26 (2018b, "The Good of Boredom"), neither of which appears in the reference list. The paper's *de facto* functional/regulatory framing is instead sourced to Danckert (2019, its own senior author) and Kurzban et al. (2013) (FE, ~p.1) — a genuine citation gap with this cluster's Elpidorou units, flagged plainly rather than papered over.

*(anticipatory-application)* On whether the reduced-attentional-control/performance-monitoring findings favor a deficit or functional/regulatory reading: the authors' own argument leans functional, without using that vocabulary. They reject "a simple mechanism of reduced attention," since a generic deficit would predict elevated misses, whereas the pattern concentrates in commission errors and two mutually uncorrelated ERPs tied to distinct executive sub-processes (context updating, performance monitoring) (FAITHFUL-Yakobi et al., ~p.9–10). Against the deficit-vs-functional axis this cluster tracks via bor-sec-25/26, this dissociable breakdown reads as more consistent with a mismatch in a particular regulatory/monitoring system than wholesale attentional degradation — our own alignment, since the authors never engage Elpidorou's framework directly *(P/anticipatory-application)*.

*(anticipatory-application)* The clean double dissociation — state boredom driving task-evoked performance/ERPs, trait BP driving only resting oscillatory power — cautions Part III against collapsing "boredom" into one measured variable: any instrument (survey item, physiological channel, or the linked VR Attention Study dataset) should specify which boredom — momentary state or stable trait — since the two correlate with non-overlapping outcomes within the same participants and session *(anticipatory-application)*.

*(anticipatory-application)* This source is a strong `grounds-measure` candidate for the not-yet-built *Boredom Experiment (VR Attention Study)* dataset on two channels: a **behavioral-performance-monitoring channel** (commission/omission rates, adaptive-difficulty titration, block-level performance-boredom correlation) and an **EEG channel** (resting alpha/theta by eyes-open/closed condition; P3/ERN amplitude). Both mappings are specific, but since the dataset entry does not exist on disk, neither can be confirmed against this source's paradigm; both bridges are flagged `****** UNVERIFIED:` *(anticipatory-application)*.

*(anticipatory-application)* Within Strand D, this source's rejection of a "simple" deficit account pairs usefully with bor-sec-27's (Barry et al.) demonstration that eyes-closed/eyes-open resting EEG is not itself a unitary "arousal" baseline: both papers argue, independently, against collapsing multi-component neurophysiological signals into a single index — a lesson for any future EEG channel, so "reduced alpha" or "reduced attention" is not treated as self-interpreting *(anticipatory-application)*.
